{"astp_version": "0.1.0", "event_type": "turn", "session": {"id": "s-f36ca6e9baad", "start_time": "2026-09-22T10:39:16.937Z", "user_id": "u-claims-0417", "tenant_id": "thornfield-mutual", "client_app": "claims-copilot", "agent_id": "astp-lab-agent-1", "config_version": "0.2.0"}, "turn": {"index": 0, "timestamp": "2026-09-22T10:39:26.225Z", "model_id": "claude-haiku-4-5", "model_version": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001", "tokens_in": 13375, "tokens_out": 104, "latency": 9.2642, "finish_reason": "tool_use"}, "content": {"prompt_text": "What guidance do we have on subsidence claims, and when should a structural engineer be instructed?", "prompt_hash": "sha256:e73401e2ca3b9a7a39aae89f6ddef5d38c5c786a60ffd906c126deebdf40ca02", "response_text": "I'll search our documents for guidance on subsidence claims and when to instruct a structural engineer.", "response_hash": "sha256:1e888618eb678e6b381076b45ace1ebb84bd351c39a196d545a5c5f6c48d3384", "system_prompt_version": "sp-0.2.0", "redaction_applied": false}, "control": {"canary_triggered": false, "policy_version": "0.1.0", "block_reason": null}}
{"astp_version": "0.1.0", "event_type": "turn", "session": {"id": "s-f36ca6e9baad", "start_time": "2026-09-22T10:39:16.937Z", "user_id": "u-claims-0417", "tenant_id": "thornfield-mutual", "client_app": "claims-copilot", "agent_id": "astp-lab-agent-1", "config_version": "0.2.0"}, "turn": {"index": 1, "timestamp": "2026-09-22T10:39:26.492Z", "model_id": "claude-haiku-4-5", "model_version": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001", "tokens_in": 14831, "tokens_out": 339, "latency": 0.2655, "finish_reason": "end_turn"}, "content": {"prompt_text": "What guidance do we have on subsidence claims, and when should a structural engineer be instructed?", "prompt_hash": "sha256:e73401e2ca3b9a7a39aae89f6ddef5d38c5c786a60ffd906c126deebdf40ca02", "response_text": "Based on our subsidence claims guidance, here's what we have:\n\n## When to instruct a structural engineer\n\n**Within five working days of notification**, you should **appoint a subsidence engineer from the panel**. This is the first step in every subsidence claim (prc-subsidence-005).\n\n## Key guidance on subsidence claims\n\n**First response priorities:**\n- Appoint a panel subsidence engineer\n- Request photographs of every crack with scale and dates noticed\n- Establish whether any tree is within a distance of the building equal to its mature height (and record species)\n- Confirm if the property has been underpinned before and obtain previous engineer's reports\n\n**Investigation:**\nThe engineer's first report gives a provisional cause and recommends either monitoring or immediate action. Where monitoring is recommended, the standard period is **twelve months** to observe seasonal ground movement across a full cycle—shorter monitoring periods cannot be relied on.\n\nSite investigation typically includes trial pits (for foundation depth), soil sampling (for plasticity), and drainage tests. The guidance notes that **drainage defects are common and cheaper to remedy than structural work**, so drains should be tested early.\n\n**Important points:**\n- Subsidence claims are long, and the early weeks set their length\n- Contact the policyholder at least monthly, even if nothing has changed\n- Underpinning is a last resort; it's not carried out where removing the cause and allowing ground recovery would work\n- Cosmetic repair is deferred until movement has stopped\n\n(prc-subsidence-005)", "response_hash": "sha256:c0a0d4b67f788f531fe62bde45d26905cff65673e233eb74af7b40bde70e2ece", "system_prompt_version": "sp-0.2.0", "redaction_applied": false}, "control": {"canary_triggered": false, "policy_version": "0.1.0", "block_reason": null}}
